+- **Distinct accessible names.** On a Polaris page, two interactive elements
+  (links, buttons, `summary` disclosures and form controls) whose targets or
+  controlled regions differ SHALL have different accessible names, as the
+  browser computes them.
+  - A link's target is its resolved `href`; a `summary`'s controlled region
+    is its `details` element; a button's or form control's is the element
+    its `aria-controls` names, or the control itself when it names none.
+  - A visible label repeated once per item or source SHALL be told apart in
+    the accessible name by the item or source it belongs to. The accessible
+    name SHALL begin with the visible label, and the words added to it name
+    the item or source by the identity PWB-REQ-014 renders for it.
+  - Two elements with the same target may share a name.
+- **Heading order.** A Polaris page SHALL have exactly one level-1 heading,
+  and in document order no heading SHALL be more than one level deeper than
+  the heading before it.
+  - The heading population is every heading element in the document after
+    load, including those inside a closed `details`, and excluding those
+    hidden by the `hidden` attribute, `aria-hidden="true"` or a computed
+    `display: none`.
+  - Headings inside a verbatim Butlers body rendered under PWB-REQ-011 are
+    counted and reported but are outside the level-1 and order rules:
+    Polaris renders them as written.
+- **Whole-surface population.** The checks in this requirement SHALL run
+  over every Polaris page served at one whole-project evaluation, including
+  the entry page and each exact-source route response PWB-REQ-011 serves.
+  Each check SHALL report the number of pages and, per page, the complete
+  interactive and heading population as its denominators. A fixture smaller
+  than that population SHALL NOT stand in for it.
+  - [Unknown] How large these populations are on Butlers' Polaris today,
+    and how many names they share: no current figure is quoted here. They
+    are measured on a fresh private-daemon capture at a named commit when
+    this is implemented.
